            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Red Flags That Should Make You Walk Away</h2>
              <h3>Immediate Deal-Breakers</h3>
              <ul class="post-list">
                <li>No contractor's license or insurance</li>
                <li>Asks you to pull your own permits</li>
                <li>Wants 50%+ upfront</li>
                <li>Can't provide references</li>
                <li>High-pressure sales tactics</li>
                <li>Can't explain their design or show samples clearly</li>
              </ul>
              <h3>Yellow Flags</h3>
              <ul class="post-list">
                <li>Less than two years in business</li>
                <li>Vague about timelines or costs</li>
                <li>Same crew working five other projects simultaneously</li>
                <li>Difficult communication during the sales phase</li>
                <li>Estimates far lower than everyone else's</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Common Mistakes Homeowners Make</h2>
              <ul class="post-list post-list--checkmark">
                <li><strong>Choosing based on price alone:</strong> Low bids often mean corner-cutting on materials and change order surprises.</li>
                <li><strong>Not getting everything in writing:</strong> Scope, specifications, payment schedule, timeline, warranties, and dispute resolution must all be in the contract.</li>
                <li><strong>Paying too much upfront:</strong> Stick to 10–20% deposit, milestone payments, and 10–15% final payment after completion.</li>
                <li><strong>Skipping reference checks:</strong> Call references and ask about budget adherence, communication, and whether they'd rehire.</li>
                <li><strong>Not planning for contingencies:</strong> Budget 10–25% extra for underlying issues, material fluctuations, and change orders.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

