                <li>Budget set with 15–20% contingency</li>
                <li>Multiple contractor reviews read</li>
                <li>Licensing and insurance requirements verified</li>
              </ul>
              <h3>Interview Phase</h3>
              <ul class="post-list post-list--check">
                <li>Spoken with 3+ contractors</li>
                <li>Asked all 14 critical questions</li>
                <li>Obtained detailed quotes</li>
                <li>References verified independently</li>
              </ul>
              <h3>Contract Phase</h3>
              <ul class="post-list post-list--check">
                <li>Reviewed complete line-item contract</li>
                <li>Agreed on payment schedule and warranty coverage</li>
                <li>Established communication plan</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

              <h3>What's the difference between a general contractor and a remodeling contractor?</h3>
              <p>General contractors handle all project types including new construction. Remodeling contractors specialize in renovation with experience in occupied homes, dust management, and privacy considerations.</p>

              <h3>Do I need building permits for my renovation project?</h3>
              <p>Permits are typically required for structural changes, electrical or plumbing work beyond simple repairs, added square footage, roof replacement, and foundation work.</p>

              <h3>What certifications should home remodeling contractors have?</h3>
              <p>Look for CGR (Certified Graduate Remodeler), LEED Certification, and NARI Membership. These indicate ongoing professional development and commitment to industry standards.</p>

              <h3>Should I get multiple quotes?</h3>
              <p>Get at least three detailed quotes. Compare experience, communication style, timeline estimates, project management approach, and overall value — not just price.</p>
            </div>

